h3 6.2 Measurements
text Counted with Anthropic's count_tokens endpoint (the public cl100k tokenizer gives the same ratios within 10%). Six surfaces: dashboard, form, table, diagram, confirmation, tabbed settings, each written with identical content in every format; A2UI is minified JSON, HTML is lean hand-written markup. Method and corpus in bench/.
table Col HTML "A2UI v0.9 JSON" "A2UI v1.0 JSON" Express Atom Elemental MX
  Output tokens to create 6 surfaces | 4,521 | 3,658 | 3,363 | 1,874 | 1,762 | 2,476 | 788
  Output tokens for 16 edits | 622 (find/replace) | 1,207 (updateComponents/updateDataModel) | – | – | – | – | 238
  Input tokens to read 6 surfaces back | 1,544 (cropped screenshots) | not possible | not possible | – | – | – | 828 (L1) / 1,246 (L2)
  Reading accuracy, Claude Haiku, 30 questions | 27/30 (screenshots) | – | – | – | – | – | 30/30
text The Express, Atom and Elemental columns are the same six surfaces written in each format with exactly the components and data of the JSON, compiled with the A2UI agent SDK's own compilers ( 0.9.0) to confirm they are valid; all 18 compile. Files in , method in  section 9.
text a2ui-agent-sdk
text bench/corpus/formats/
link bench/results.md results.html
text Writability: three models (Claude Haiku, Sonnet, Opus) given only the spec wrote 12 of 12 requested surfaces and patches validly, and produced byte-identical patches for the same edit.
text On this corpus Express is 44% below v1.0 JSON (its document claims 55–70%), Atom is 6% below Express (its document claims 25–50%), and Elemental is 26% below JSON. MX is 2.2× smaller than Atom, the smallest of the three. The difference is design rather than syntax: the three formats keep A2UI's model (an id or variable per node, a separately written data model, two nodes per button, options as objects), and MX does not (section 6.3).
h3 6.3 What MX adds that the other formats do not
text Patches with derived ids. Express/Atom/Elemental re-send components by id; the model must have chosen and remembered the ids. MX derives them from content, so a patch is *revenue $315k and the model needs no memory of the creation.
text Read-back in the same syntax. observe returns MX; the model can copy a line, change a word and send it as a patch.
text "Lifecycle words on the surface line: @confirm until:action ttl:60s residue:line, compiled to section 4."
text Revision on every patch: ~3, compiled to section 5.
text What it lacks: templates over lists (_template in Express), client functions beyond the fixed validation set, and per-component catalog ids. These are deliberate omissions for the first version, not conflicts.
h2 7. Reference implementation
text In this repository:
text packages/core: MX parser, patcher, perception (levels, boxes, diagnostics, since diffs) and the A2UI compiler for v0.9 and v1.0, with lifecycle metadata and the surfaceDissolved action. 23 tests, parity-tested against the Python reference used for the benchmark.
text "packages/renderer: a DOM renderer that implements lifecycle (ttl, seen, until, ephemeral, residues), events with revisions, layout boxes from getBoundingClientRect, and the four diagnostics. It renders MX directly, not A2UI JSON; its observe is the function of section 3 with format: \"mx\"."
text packages/mcp: an MCP server exposing render / patch / observe / look / dissolve, which is how we dogfood it from a coding agent.
text To make this an A2UI renderer feature rather than a parallel renderer, the perception module needs to be bound to an A2UI renderer's component tree. For @a2ui/lit or @a2ui/react that is: walk the surface's rendered components, read each element's getBoundingClientRect, compute the four diagnostics from the DOM, serialise with the current data model. The core's perceive() already does the serialisation and the diff from a tree plus a box map; the renderer-specific part is about 150 lines. Lifecycle is a timer and an IntersectionObserver per surface, about 80 lines in our renderer (packages/renderer/src/host.ts).
h2 8. Compatibility
text Nothing here changes the agent-to-renderer or renderer-to-agent schema except the optional metadata on update messages in section 5.
text Renderers that do not implement the extensions ignore them, as v1.0 requires for unknown extensions keys.
text Agents detect support from inlineCatalogs (perception) and may send lifecycle metadata blind; a surface without lifecycle support simply persists, which is today's behaviour.
text All of it works on v0.9.1 too, except that v0.9.1 has no metadata.extensions on createSurface; there the lifecycle object would go in theme or be dropped.
h2 9. Open questions for the maintainers
text Function or message for perception (3.5)? We prefer the function for zero schema change; a message is cleaner long-term.
text Should the level 2 box be in CSS pixels or density-independent units? We use CSS pixels because the diagnostics thresholds (24 px targets, WCAG ratios) are defined in them.
text Is the a2ui_ prefix available for lifecycle and revision if the design is accepted, so agents can rely on one name?
text Is there interest in a shared corpus for inference-format token measurements? We offer bench/corpus and the counting scripts.
h2 10. Before sending
text "The benchmark's custom catalog id is still https://manifest.dev/bench/catalog.json, a placeholder kept so the recorded token counts stay reproducible. The catalog itself is bench/catalog.json; hosting it at the project site and changing the id moves the A2UI counts by a few tokens per surface."
text Manifest is Apache-2.0.
link "Source, issues and discussion on GitHub." https://github.com/brunopgalvao/manifest
